#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168086 :tutorial/content "Recall that if the random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed over the interval $[a,b]$, then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{b - a},\\begin{vmatrix}a \\le x \\le b \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that the expected value and variance of $X$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= \\frac{a + b}{2} \\\\\nVar[X] &= ((b - a)^{2})/(12)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result for $\\textrm{E}[X]$ is intuitive as it's simply the midpoint of the interval $[a,b]$.\n\nWe will derive both of these results at the end of the lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "b5aec157-2481-5e36-90cf-d794324c0d63" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5171 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168111 :tutorial/content "If $X\\sim U[a,b]$, then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{b - a},\\begin{vmatrix}a \\le x \\le b \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nWe can compute the formula for $\\textrm E[X]$ using the usual definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= \\int_{a}^{b}xf(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{a}^{b}\\frac{x}{b - a}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{b - a}\\int_{a}^{b}xdx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{b - a} \\cdot \\left.\\frac{1}{2}x^{2}\\right|_{a}^{b} \\\\\n&= (1)/(2(b - a)) \\cdot (b^{2} - a^{2}) \\\\\n&= (1)/(2(b - a)) \\cdot (b - a)(b + a) \\\\\n&= (1)/(2(b - a)) \\cdot (b - a)(b + a) \\\\\n&= \\frac{a + b}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1bdf6fbe-b1ad-56fa-a9f6-e9f99b79f3d0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5374 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Formula for the Mean"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168112 :tutorial/content "If $X\\sim U[a,b]$, then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{b - a},\\begin{vmatrix}a \\le x \\le b \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nTo compute $\\textrm{Var}[X]$, we use the relation\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - (\\textrm{E}[X])^2\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $\\textrm E[X^2]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X^{2}] &= \\int_{a}^{b}x^{2}f(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{a}^{b}\\frac{x^{2}}{b - a}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{b - a}\\int_{a}^{b}x^{2}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{b - a} \\cdot \\left.\\frac{1}{3}x^{3}\\right|_{a}^{b} \\\\\n&= (1)/(3(b - a)) \\cdot (b^{3} - a^{3}) \\\\\n&= (1)/(3(b - a)) \\cdot (b - a)(b^{2} + ab + a^{2}) \\\\\n&= (1)/(3(b - a)) \\cdot (b - a)(b^{2} + ab + a^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(b^{2} + ab + a^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\dfrac12(b+a)\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n(\\textrm E[X])^2 = \\dfrac14(b^2+2ab+a^2)\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X] &= E[X^{2}] - (E[X])^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(b^{2} + ab + a^{2}) - \\frac{1}{4}(b^{2} + 2ab + a^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{12}(b^{2} + ab + a^{2}) - \\frac{3}{12}(b^{2} + 2ab + a^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12}(4b^{2} + 4ab + 4a^{2}) - \\frac{1}{12}(3b^{2} + 6ab + 3a^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12}(b^{2} - 2ab + a^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12}(b - a)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "16d85ab9-dd16-51a2-97f0-2ca5ef2e3d08" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5375 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Formula for the Variance"}]}
